1. A method for processing fermented or incubated cocoa beans, the method comprising:

   (a) providing fermented or incubated cocoa beans comprising cocoa solids, cocoa fat, water, and acetic acid;

   (b) comminuting the fermented or incubated cocoa beans to form a cocoa bean mass;

   (c) subjecting the cocoa bean mass to a heat treatment at a temperature low enough to substantially avoid roasting of the cocoa bean mass while liquefying at least a portion of the cocoa fat and promoting partitioning of acetic acid into an aqueous phase;

   (d) separating the heat-treated cocoa bean mass into at least three phases comprising:  
   (i) a water phase comprising water and acetic acid,  
   (ii) a fat phase comprising cocoa fat, and  
   (iii) a solid phase comprising cocoa solids; and

   (e) removing at least a portion of the acetic acid from the cocoa beans by removing the water phase comprising the acetic acid,

   thereby producing separated cocoa-derived water, fat, and solid fractions having a reduced acetic acid content relative to the fermented or incubated cocoa beans.
